1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,525
This video is about grammars. A grammar is a finite way to write down a language.

2
00:00:05,975 --> 00:00:20,354
We learn the parts of a grammar, how a derivation works, and how to draw a parse tree. We also learn the classes of grammars, from regular to unrestricted. At the end we solve three question bank questions.

3
00:00:20,804 --> 00:00:29,657
The compiler front end has two stages. The lexer cuts the characters into tokens. The parser groups the tokens into a tree.

4
00:00:30,107 --> 00:00:36,699
For the lexer, the specification is a set of regular expressions. The machine is a D F A.

5
00:00:37,149 --> 00:00:50,611
For the parser, the specification is a grammar. Regular expressions cannot describe nested structure, such as nested parentheses. A grammar can. This chapter of the notes is about grammars.

6
00:00:51,061 --> 00:00:59,402
Start with the basic idea. A language is a set of strings. Here the set has three strings: a, a b, and a b c.

7
00:00:59,852 --> 00:01:06,615
A recognizer is a program that checks if an input string is in the set. It answers yes or no.

8
00:01:07,065 --> 00:01:13,529
If the language is finite, we can write all its strings down. This example has only three.

9
00:01:13,979 --> 00:01:29,979
But almost all languages are infinite. We cannot list an infinite set in finite space and time. So we need a finite notation. The notes list four: grammars, regular expressions, set comprehensions, and words.

10
00:01:30,429 --> 00:01:41,479
Here are three languages from the notes, each in four notations. Language 1 is any number of x's. Language 2 is an x followed by some y's.

11
00:01:41,929 --> 00:01:53,748
As regular expressions: x star, and x y plus. As set comprehensions: x to the n for n at least 0, and x y to the n for n at least 1.

12
00:01:54,198 --> 00:02:10,433
As grammars. Language 1: S goes to x S, or epsilon. Language 2: S goes to x y T, and T goes to y T or epsilon. A grammar repeats with recursion: S appears on its own right side.

13
00:02:10,883 --> 00:02:29,357
Language 3 counts two things: n x's, then the same number of y's. A set comprehension and a grammar can describe it. A regular expression cannot, because a regex cannot count. Some languages are impossible to specify in some notations.

14
00:02:29,807 --> 00:02:39,237
Look at the grammar for language 2 in detail. It has two rules. S goes to x y T. T goes to y T, or epsilon.

15
00:02:39,687 --> 00:02:50,652
S and T are nonterminals. A nonterminal stands for a part of the language. It must be rewritten. In the notes, nonterminals start with a capital letter.

16
00:02:51,102 --> 00:02:57,865
x and y are terminals. Terminals are the tokens that come from the lexer. They are never rewritten.

17
00:02:58,315 --> 00:03:10,965
Each rule is a production. It has a left hand side, an arrow, and a right hand side. The bar separates alternatives for the same left hand side. Epsilon is the empty string.

18
00:03:11,415 --> 00:03:18,349
The start symbol is the left hand side of the first rule. Here it is S. Every derivation starts there.

19
00:03:18,799 --> 00:03:34,457
Now use a grammar. A derivation starts with the start symbol and rewrites one nonterminal per step, with one production. Derive x x x with the grammar S goes to x S, or epsilon. Line 1 is S.

20
00:03:34,907 --> 00:03:44,507
Rewrite S with S goes to x S. The new symbols are orange. On the right, the parse tree grows: S gets two children, x and S.

21
00:03:44,957 --> 00:03:49,437
Rewrite the new S, again with x S. Now we have x x S.

22
00:03:49,887 --> 00:03:51,999
Once more. x x x S.

23
00:03:52,449 --> 00:04:10,433
Now choose the other alternative, S goes to epsilon. The S disappears. The string x x x has no nonterminals left, so x x x is in the language. The parse tree shows which rule made which part. Its leaves, read left to right, are the string.

24
00:04:10,883 --> 00:04:16,707
Another one. Derive x y y with the grammar for language 2. Start with S.

25
00:04:17,157 --> 00:04:24,603
S has only one production. S goes to x y T. Two terminals appear, and one nonterminal, T.

26
00:04:25,053 --> 00:04:30,215
The input still has one more y. So rewrite T with T goes to y T.

27
00:04:30,665 --> 00:04:39,177
Now the input is finished. Rewrite T with epsilon. The result is x y y, so x y y is in the language.

28
00:04:39,627 --> 00:04:47,521
Language 3: S goes to x S y, or x y, or epsilon. Derive x x x y y y.

29
00:04:47,971 --> 00:04:53,901
S goes to x S y. One x on the left, one y on the right, and S in the middle.

30
00:04:54,351 --> 00:04:59,194
Again, S goes to x S y. Now there are two x's and two y's.

31
00:04:59,644 --> 00:05:24,647
Last, S goes to x y. The result is three x's and three y's. Each step adds one x and one y at the same time. That is how a grammar counts. The tree is nested: each S is inside the one above it. A second derivation also exists: x S y, then epsilon. Two derivations for one string is ambiguity. Video 18 covers it.

32
00:05:25,097 --> 00:05:46,814
Language 5 in the notes is nested subtractions. S goes to E. E goes to E minus T, or T. T goes to an expression in parentheses, or an integer. Here is the tree for 9 minus, open parenthesis, 5 minus 2, close parenthesis. At the top, E becomes E minus T.

33
00:05:47,264 --> 00:05:54,795
The left E becomes T, then the integer 9. The right T becomes parentheses with a new E inside.

34
00:05:55,245 --> 00:06:09,751
Inside the parentheses, the same rules repeat: E minus T, then 5 and 2. An E inside a T inside an E. Nesting to any depth is one of the things a regular expression cannot do.

35
00:06:10,201 --> 00:06:25,412
Grammars are classified by complexity. This is the Chomsky hierarchy from the notes. The outside class is unrestricted grammars. To parse them, you need a Turing machine, and the time has no reasonable limit.

36
00:06:25,862 --> 00:06:31,451
Inside are the context-sensitive grammars. They need a linear bounded automaton.

37
00:06:31,901 --> 00:06:42,525
Inside those are the context-free grammars, C F Gs. A nondeterministic pushdown automaton parses them in order n cubed time.

38
00:06:42,975 --> 00:07:00,789
Inside the context-free class is L L one. You can write an L L one parser by hand with recursive descent. It runs in linear time. P E G, parsing expression grammars, overlap with L L one. They come later in the course.

39
00:07:01,239 --> 00:07:12,631
In the centre are the regular grammars. A finite automaton parses them in linear time. This course works mostly with regular, L L one and context-free.

40
00:07:13,081 --> 00:07:24,323
When you design a language, always choose the simplest possible class. Simpler grammars take less time and less machinery to parse. The notes call this the kiss rule.

41
00:07:24,773 --> 00:07:35,419
Engineers do not always follow it. The full grammar for V H D L is at least ambiguous. a of i can be an array access or a function call.

42
00:07:35,869 --> 00:07:50,759
The language that the airline industry uses to encode ticket information uses an unrestricted grammar. It was designed before grammar complexity was well understood, and it still causes trouble for engineers.

43
00:07:51,209 --> 00:08:04,841
Two grammars can describe the same language. Here are two grammars for x star. The left one is right recursive: S goes to x S. The right one is left recursive: S goes to S x.

44
00:08:05,291 --> 00:08:16,705
Both grammars give the same set of strings. A python check compared all strings up to length 8. Two grammars that accept exactly the same strings are weakly equivalent.

45
00:08:17,155 --> 00:08:24,941
But the parse trees are different. For x x, the left tree leans to the right, and the right tree leans to the left.

46
00:08:25,391 --> 00:08:39,727
Two grammars that give the same strings and the same parse trees are strongly equivalent. These two are only weakly equivalent. Engineers often refactor a complex grammar into an equivalent, simpler one.

47
00:08:40,177 --> 00:08:52,167
How do we test if a grammar is context free? Here is a grammar from the notes for a to the n, b to the n, c to the n. That is n a's, then n b's, then n c's.

48
00:08:52,617 --> 00:09:04,819
The test: a context-free grammar has exactly one nonterminal on the left hand side of every production. The top two rules pass. They rewrite S, wherever it appears.

49
00:09:05,269 --> 00:09:21,803
All the other rules have two symbols on the left. C B goes to H B. a B goes to a b. The rule a B can rewrite B only when B comes right after an a. The context decides, so the grammar is context sensitive.

50
00:09:22,253 --> 00:09:35,351
Multiple symbols on the left hand side mean the grammar is not context free. The canonical example is this language, which counts three things. No context-free grammar exists for it.

51
00:09:35,801 --> 00:09:47,855
Here is a full derivation of a a b b c c with this grammar. S goes to a S B C. Then S goes to a B C. Now we have two a's, and B's and C's mixed.

52
00:09:48,305 --> 00:09:54,385
The C's must move to the right of the B's. The rule C B goes to H B starts a swap.

53
00:09:54,835 --> 00:10:03,347
H B goes to H C, and H C goes to B C. The three rules together swap C and B. Now the B's are together.

54
00:10:03,797 --> 00:10:11,349
a B goes to a b, then b B goes to b b. Each B becomes a b only after an a or a b.

55
00:10:11,799 --> 00:10:40,471
b C goes to b c, and c C goes to c c. The result is a a b b c c. Our python search found a flaw in this figure. It also derives a a a b b c c c c, because one H can help in two swaps. The source that the notes cite uses two helpers, Z and W. That version derives only a to the n, b to the n, c to the n. For the exam, the important point is the test: several symbols on a left hand side.

56
00:10:40,921 --> 00:10:49,902
Is a grammar regular? First, the three common cases that are outside the regular class. Engineers need all three in practice.

57
00:10:50,352 --> 00:10:57,435
One. Unlimited balanced parentheses. n open parentheses, an a, then n close parentheses.

58
00:10:57,885 --> 00:11:05,970
Two. Expression nesting. An expression can contain an expression in parentheses, or two expressions with a plus.

59
00:11:06,420 --> 00:11:13,076
Three. Indefinite counting. n a's followed by n b's. S goes to a S b, or a b.

60
00:11:13,526 --> 00:11:29,227
These are the three main features to look for. Each one needs a count with no limit. A finite automaton has a finite number of states, so it cannot keep such a count. If the language needs one of them, the grammar is not regular.

61
00:11:29,677 --> 00:11:43,331
Now the test to prove that a grammar is regular. The left hand side must be one nonterminal, as in a context-free grammar. The right hand side must be the empty string, or a single terminal.

62
00:11:43,781 --> 00:12:04,090
Or it must be linear. Right linear is a terminal followed by a nonterminal, such as x S. Left linear is a nonterminal followed by a terminal, such as S x. All the rules must be right linear, or all must be left linear. If the two forms are mixed, the grammar might not be regular.

63
00:12:04,540 --> 00:12:14,012
Test the grammar for language 2. The rule S goes to x y T has two terminals before the nonterminal. That is not one of the forms.

64
00:12:14,462 --> 00:12:30,377
Refactor it. Add a nonterminal U. S goes to x U, and U goes to y T. Now every rule is right linear, so the grammar is regular. A python check showed that both grammars give the same strings up to length 8.

65
00:12:30,827 --> 00:12:44,608
Language 3 has S goes to x S y. The nonterminal is in the middle, with terminals on both sides. That is not linear. The language counts two things, so no regular grammar exists for it.

66
00:12:45,058 --> 00:12:57,751
Here is our own example of why the two forms must not be mixed. S goes to a T, or epsilon. T goes to S b. The first rule is right linear. The second is left linear.

67
00:12:58,201 --> 00:13:06,265
Derive a a b b. S goes to a T. T goes to S b. Now S is in the middle again, between a and b.

68
00:13:06,715 --> 00:13:20,987
Repeat once, then use epsilon. The result is a a b b. This grammar gives n a's then n b's. A python check confirmed it up to length 8. So every rule is linear, but the mix is not regular.

69
00:13:21,437 --> 00:13:39,293
Now the question bank. Question 1.1.1. Engineers work in both measurement systems. Using this grammar, derive metric 3.875, and imperial 3 7 slash 8. Digits is one or more digits, from the lexer.

70
00:13:39,743 --> 00:13:41,386
Pause the video and try it.

71
00:13:41,836 --> 00:13:48,620
The lexer gives four tokens: metric, 3, dot, 875. Start with Measurement.

72
00:13:49,070 --> 00:13:56,110
The first token is metric. So use the first alternative: Measurement goes to metric DecimalNumber.

73
00:13:56,560 --> 00:14:12,987
DecimalNumber has one production: Digits dot Digits. Now there are two nonterminals. We rewrite the leftmost one first. This is a leftmost derivation. The L L in L L one means left to right, leftmost derivation.

74
00:14:13,437 --> 00:14:16,359
The leftmost Digits matches the token 3.

75
00:14:16,809 --> 00:14:26,260
The last Digits matches 875. No nonterminals are left, and the string matches the input. The derivation accepts.

76
00:14:26,710 --> 00:14:36,502
Now imperial 3 7 slash 8. Five tokens. The first token is imperial, so Measurement goes to imperial FractionalNumber.

77
00:14:36,952 --> 00:14:44,739
FractionalNumber goes to Digits Digits slash Digits. The space between 3 and 7 separates two tokens.

78
00:14:45,189 --> 00:14:58,266
Rewrite each Digits, leftmost first: 3, then 7, then 8. The result matches the input. The posted solution does the three Digits steps in one line. That is fine on an exam.

79
00:14:58,716 --> 00:15:14,311
Question 1.1.2. Try to derive five sentences with this grammar. The parentheses group two alternatives. For example, Technical is Engineer calculates, or Architect draws, followed by design.

80
00:15:14,761 --> 00:15:16,403
Pause the video and try it.

81
00:15:16,853 --> 00:15:25,237
Engineer calculates design. ProfessionalSkills goes to Technical. Technical goes to Engineer calculates design.

82
00:15:25,687 --> 00:15:32,429
Physician examines patient. ProfessionalSkills goes to Medical, then Physician examines patient.

83
00:15:32,879 --> 00:15:38,831
Judge decides case. ProfessionalSkills goes to Legal, then Judge decides case.

84
00:15:39,281 --> 00:15:44,166
Each accepted sentence takes two steps. The first word decides the group.

85
00:15:44,616 --> 00:15:52,595
Engineer cuts patient. The word Engineer appears only in Technical. So ProfessionalSkills must go to Technical.

86
00:15:53,045 --> 00:15:58,911
Technical goes to Engineer calculates design. Engineer matches the first token.

87
00:15:59,361 --> 00:16:04,844
The grammar needs calculates next. The input has cuts. The derivation is stuck.

88
00:16:05,294 --> 00:16:16,174
Lawyer advocates design. Lawyer appears only in Legal. Lawyer and advocates match. Then Legal needs case, but the input has design. Stuck again.

89
00:16:16,624 --> 00:16:29,467
When no derivation reaches the input, the string is not in the language. The posted solution writes stuck, reject. Surgeon cuts patient, and Lawyer advocates case, would be accepted.

90
00:16:29,917 --> 00:16:49,031
Question 1.2.1. A simple calculator language with operator precedence and nested expressions. Derive five strings: 1 plus 2, 2 times 3, 1 plus 2 times 3, open 1 plus 2 close times 3, and 1 times 2 plus 3.

91
00:16:49,481 --> 00:16:51,124
Pause the video and try it.

92
00:16:51,574 --> 00:16:57,291
First see how the grammar encodes precedence. Expr is a sum of one or more Terms.

93
00:16:57,741 --> 00:17:00,749
Term is a product of one or more Factors.

94
00:17:01,199 --> 00:17:17,626
Factor is an integer, or a whole Expr in parentheses. Each level of precedence is its own nonterminal. Times is one level deeper than plus. So in every parse tree, a times sits below the plus nodes, and is done first.

95
00:17:18,076 --> 00:17:21,212
Start with 1 plus 2. S goes to Expr.

96
00:17:21,662 --> 00:17:29,513
The input has a plus. So Expr goes to Term plus Expr. Then the left Term goes to Factor, and Factor to Int.

97
00:17:29,963 --> 00:17:38,795
Int is the token 1. Then the right Expr has no more plus, so it goes to Term, Term goes to Factor, and Factor goes to Int.

98
00:17:39,245 --> 00:17:48,610
The last Int is the token 2. The derivation reaches 1 plus 2. On the right, the tree has plus at the top, with 1 and 2 below.

99
00:17:49,060 --> 00:17:54,415
2 times 3. S goes to Expr. There is no plus, so Expr goes to Term.

100
00:17:54,865 --> 00:18:01,521
There is a times. So Term goes to Factor times Term. The left Factor goes to Int, which is 2.

101
00:18:01,971 --> 00:18:08,456
The right Term goes to Factor, then Int, then 3. The derivation reaches 2 times 3.

102
00:18:08,906 --> 00:18:16,735
1 plus 2 times 3. S goes to Expr. There is a plus in the input, so Expr goes to Term plus Expr.

103
00:18:17,185 --> 00:18:23,009
The left Term has no times before the plus. It goes to Factor, then Int, then 1.

104
00:18:23,459 --> 00:18:31,971
The right Expr must derive 2 times 3. It has no plus, so it goes to Term. That Term goes to Factor times Term.

105
00:18:32,421 --> 00:18:36,816
The Factor goes to Int, then 2. The last Term goes to Factor.

106
00:18:37,266 --> 00:18:52,007
Then Int, and 3. With brackets, the result is 1 plus, bracket, 2 times 3. In the tree, the times is inside the right Expr, below the plus. So the multiplication is done first: 1 plus 6 is 7.

107
00:18:52,457 --> 00:18:58,260
1 times 2 plus 3. S goes to Expr. Expr goes to Term plus Expr.

108
00:18:58,710 --> 00:19:04,235
Now the left Term must derive 1 times 2. Term goes to Factor times Term.

109
00:19:04,685 --> 00:19:10,893
Factor goes to Int, then 1. The inner Term goes to Factor. It must stop before the plus.

110
00:19:11,343 --> 00:19:17,829
Then Int, then 2. The left side is complete: 1 times 2. The right Expr goes to Term.

111
00:19:18,279 --> 00:19:29,052
Term goes to Factor, Int, then 3. With brackets: bracket 1 times 2, plus 3. The times is again below the plus. 2 plus 3 is 5.

112
00:19:29,502 --> 00:19:40,958
Open 1 plus 2 close, times 3. S goes to Expr. The plus is inside the parentheses, so Expr goes to Term. Term goes to Factor times Term.

113
00:19:41,408 --> 00:19:50,624
This Factor must derive the part in parentheses. Factor goes to open, Expr, close. The inner Expr goes to Term plus Expr.

114
00:19:51,074 --> 00:19:58,221
Inside the parentheses, the rules repeat. Term, Factor, Int, 1. Then Expr, Term, Factor, Int.

115
00:19:58,671 --> 00:20:03,940
That Int is 2. Then the last Term after the times goes to Factor, then Int.

116
00:20:04,390 --> 00:20:14,545
The last Int is 3. Now the plus is below the times, because the parentheses put a whole Expr inside a Factor. 3 times 3 is 9.

117
00:20:14,995 --> 00:20:22,888
Compare with the posted solution. In 2 times 3 and in 1 times 2 plus 3, it writes Factor times Expr.

118
00:20:23,338 --> 00:20:33,983
The grammar has no such production. It says Term goes to Factor times Term. Our derivations use the grammar exactly, and a python check found each one.

119
00:20:34,433 --> 00:20:49,004
Some posted lines also do two rewrites at once. The posted solution says that on an exam you may skip the steps that only move down the grammar to an Int. But every step you write must use a real production.

120
00:20:49,454 --> 00:20:57,027
To summarize. A language is a set of strings. A grammar describes an infinite language in finite space.

121
00:20:57,477 --> 00:21:06,736
Nonterminals are rewritten. Terminals are the tokens. A derivation starts at the start symbol and uses one production per step.

122
00:21:07,186 --> 00:21:15,314
The parse tree records each rewrite. In a leftmost derivation, you always rewrite the leftmost nonterminal first.

123
00:21:15,764 --> 00:21:24,788
The classes nest: regular, L L one, context free, context sensitive, unrestricted. Always choose the simplest class.

124
00:21:25,238 --> 00:21:43,457
A context-free grammar has one nonterminal on every left hand side. A regular grammar is all right linear or all left linear. Counting, nesting and balanced parentheses are not regular. Next video: recursive descent, which turns a grammar into code.
